     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose looks unsafe.**\n- Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. That is twice the usual maximum of 2,550 mg/day (about 2,000\u20132,550 mg/day depending on the formulation).\n- A single 1,700 mg dose is also well above the usual per-dose maximum of about 850\u20131,000 mg.\n- An overdose raises the risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n- The number may be a typo. Common regimens are 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg/day) or 1,000 mg twice daily.\n\n**2. You're not a clinician.** A prescriber or other authorized clinician should place or verify medication orders. That way the dose and the patient details have been checked by someone licensed to do so.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- Ask the prescriber to confirm the intended dose. If it was meant to be 850 mg, 500 mg, or similar, I can enter it once it's confirmed.\n- Or have the prescriber enter the order directly.\n\nIf the prescriber confirms they really intend 1700 mg three times daily, send me that confirmation and I'll reconsider. I'd add a note on the order flagging that the dose exceeds the usual maximum."
      }
     ],
